>
 <p>
  Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ is Lozen Advisory’s methodology for identifying systemic risks that legacy measurement systems are structurally blind to.
</p>

<p>
  The category exists because organizations are now making consequential decisions from evidence systems that were not built for the risks they are being asked to govern. Workforce systems often miss what employees cannot safely disclose. AI governance systems often miss who reviewed, verified, escalated, refused, or signed off on AI-assisted output. Large language models and agentic AI systems compound the problem by acting autonomously inside enterprise workflows, while creating an accountability gap and decision debt.
</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>
    What risk is the organization harboring because the current system was not designed to detect complex interdependencies and emergent technology threats?
  </p>
</blockquote>

<p>
  This page defines the governance architecture Lozen Advisory uses to evaluate that gap. It connects workforce non-disclosure, hidden AI use, undocumented human review, vendor/deployer exposure, agentic AI, and board evidence into a single question: can the institution prove who knew, who acted, who reviewed, who refused, and who remains accountable when the formal record is incomplete?
</p>

<p>
  Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ gives leaders a vocabulary for risks legacy systems do not yet classify: disclosure-dependent risk, unmeasured human capacity, institutional opacity, autonomous agentic systems as enterprise actors, hidden verification labor, and human accountability under the Name Standard℠.
</p>

<p>
  The methodology operates through Lozen Advisory’s core frameworks, including Invisible Attrition℠, Tacere, the Power User Trap℠, the Name Standard℠, and the 5Ws of Decision Integrity℠. Together, they evaluate whether accountability is real, documented, and defensible — not merely assigned in policy.
</p>

  <h2>The disclosure gap across workforce and AI governance</h2>

  <p>
    Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ operates across two domains where institutional records routinely fail: workforce risk and AI governance.
  </p>

  <p>
    In workforce systems, the missing evidence may be silent self-management, non-use of benefits, suppressed disclosure, informal accommodation, or leadership attrition before detection. The organization may see stable output while missing the capacity strain holding that output together.
  </p>

  <p>
    In AI governance, the missing evidence may be undocumented human review, unclear sign-off authority, hidden verification labor, vendor-embedded AI, shadow AI, agentic workflows, or AI-generated records without traceable accountability. The organization may see a completed record while missing how the record was produced, reviewed, corrected, or authorized.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both domains expose the same governance failure: the institution treats invisible labor, silence, and undocumented accountability as operationally neutral because legacy systems were not designed to classify them.
  </p>

  <p>
    They are not neutral. When leaders cannot see the hidden labor, silence, judgment, review work, or accountability structure holding a system together, they cannot accurately govern retention risk, AI adoption, board evidence, compliance exposure, or institutional capacity.
  </p>

  <h2>A board concern: governance quality under investor scrutiny</h2>

  <p>
    Boards face converging pressure from investors, proxy advisors, insurers, and regulators to demonstrate that AI oversight is substantive — not merely stated. Proxy statements, 10-K risk factors, and annual reports are increasingly machine-readable evidence surfaces: investor-side AI, proxy advisors, governance-risk reviewers, and litigation-adjacent scrutiny can test whether AI oversight language contains actual accountability evidence or only process claims. The U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission has made clear that overstated AI capability claims invite the same scrutiny as overstated financial performance.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      A proxy statement can disclose that AI governance exists without disclosing whether anyone could be held accountable for it.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    That is not a compliance problem. It is a Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ problem. A board can accurately state that a human-oversight policy exists while having no way to produce evidence that the human in that role had the authority, information, and support to make the sign-off defensible. Disclosure of a process is not evidence that the process produced accountability.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is also a D&amp;O exposure question. Governance-risk underwriters are increasingly asking about AI governance quality, not just AI governance existence. A board that can point to a policy but not to a producible accountability record is disclosing a control that may not survive examination.
  </p>

<h3>Proxy Name Standard℠ Diagnostic</h3>

<p>
  Lozen Advisory is developing the <strong>Proxy Name Standard℠ Diagnostic</strong>, an AI-powered tool that analyzes governance disclosure language from publicly filed proxy statements and identifies where human-accountability evidence gaps may be surfaced by investor-side AI, proxy advisors, governance-risk reviewers, or litigation-adjacent scrutiny.
</p>

<p>
  The diagnostic applies the Name Standard℠ to proxy language, distinguishing statements that merely describe AI oversight from statements that produce evidence of accountable human review, authority, escalation, refusal, and sign-off.
</p>

<p>
  The tool is organized around four diagnostic categories: weak or unsupported governance language; buried or footnoted disclosure gaps; tone signals that obscure accountability; and context gaps between AI capability claims and the evidence supporting them.
</p>

<p>
  The public beta will evaluate post-filing proxy language available from public sources. A separate internal portal will support operator-only review of documents Lozen Advisory is authorized to evaluate, including draft governance language, board materials, and other non-public documents provided for advisory review.
</p>

<p>
  Until the diagnostic is published, leadership teams preparing proxy or risk-factor language on AI governance can request a Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ review of draft disclosure language before it is filed.
</p>


<h2>Why legacy governance processes miss the risk</h2>

<p>
  AI has existed inside large enterprises for years. That is not the change. The change is the arrival of large language models and agentic AI systems that do not behave like ordinary enterprise software.
</p>

<p>
  Legacy governance systems were built around visible processes, formal roles, declared issues, access controls, approval chains, and documented decisions. They assume that work is performed by employees, contractors, vendors, or systems whose functions are defined in advance.
</p>

<p>
  LLMs disrupt that assumption. Anthropic’s agent research describes augmented LLMs as systems connected to tools, retrieval, and memory that can generate search queries, select tools, and determine what information to retain. In enterprise use, that means LLMs can generate language, summarize evidence, draft records, classify information, select tools, and execute multi-step tasks inside ordinary workflows. When connected to retrieval systems, memory, software tools, or enterprise applications, they no longer function only as passive software. They begin to operate as delegated actors inside the organization.
</p>

<p>
  That creates a governance problem legacy systems were not designed to classify. The organization may see a document, analysis, email, recommendation, code change, meeting summary, customer response, or internal record. It may not be able to see how much of that output was produced by a human, how much was generated by an AI system, what source material the system relied on, what judgment the human actually exercised, or who is accountable for the final result.
</p>

<p>
  In practice, LLMs can become a form of ghost labor inside the enterprise: producing work, shaping records, absorbing tasks, and influencing decisions without appearing in the organizational chart, delegation model, performance system, or accountability record.
</p>

<p>
  Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ exists because legacy systems are not built for that operating environment. They were designed to govern declared tools and visible actors. They are not sufficient for a workplace where AI-assisted output, human review, vendor-deployed AI, agentic workflows, informal workarounds, and undocumented verification labor can all shape the institutional record before the board ever sees it.
</p>

  <h2>Pillar One: Invisible Attrition℠</h2>

  <p>
    Lozen Advisory’s work on <strong>Invisible Attrition℠</strong> examines leadership capacity erosion before organizational detection.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is especially visible in menopause-at-work, where organizations often rely on women to self-manage symptoms, workload, disclosure risk, stigma, and career consequences without producing data the organization can accurately interpret.
  </p>

  <p>
    Low benefits utilization does not necessarily mean low need. Stable performance does not necessarily mean stable capacity. Silence does not necessarily mean absence of risk.
  </p>

  <p>
    Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ evaluates the gap between what the organization can measure and what the workforce may be absorbing without disclosure.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Retention risk that does not appear in exit data until after loss</li>
    <li>Leadership pipeline fragility masked by continued performance</li>
    <li>Benefits designs that require disclosure before support can activate</li>
    <li>Workforce issues treated as individual health matters despite operational consequences</li>
    <li>Data systems that confuse non-use with non-need</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The governance question is not only whether support exists. The question is whether the organization can detect risk when the people most affected have rational reasons not to disclose it. Boards cannot govern a leadership continuity risk that management’s measurement systems were not designed to surface.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same evidence failure operates inside AI governance — in a different domain but through identical structural logic.
  </p>

  <h2>Pillar Two: The Name Standard℠</h2>

<p>
  Lozen Advisory’s work on undocumented AI decision-making examines what happens when AI-assisted systems influence corporate output, but human accountability remains unclear, informal, unsupported, or hidden from the institutional record.
</p>

<p>
  AI governance has produced two dominant placement models. Human-in-the-loop places a person in the decision process before output or action is finalized. Human-on-the-loop places a person in a monitoring position, with the ability to intervene after or during operation. Both are workflow design positions.
</p>

<p>
  Neither model answers the board-level attribution question.
</p>

<p>
  The real question is not where the human sits in the workflow. The question is whether the organization can prove who relied on AI, what the AI system produced, what the human reviewed, what was escalated, what was refused, what was corrected, and whose authority attached to the final output.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Human-in-the-loop and human-on-the-loop models describe placement. The Name Standard℠ evaluates accountability.</strong>
</p>

<p>
  That distinction matters because AI-assisted work can now enter the organization through approved tools, vendor systems, embedded software, employee workarounds, and unapproved consumer AI. The formal record may show a human-authored memo, analysis, recommendation, email, code change, meeting summary, or board material. The underlying work may have been drafted, summarized, classified, rewritten, or materially shaped by AI.
</p>

<p>
  Shadow AI turns that attribution problem into a governance problem. A 2026 TrustedTech / Censuswide study found that 48% of UK and US employees use unapproved AI tools at work. The pattern is most concentrated at the top: 65% of decision-makers reported using Shadow AI, compared with 31% below decision-maker level, and 72.8% of C-level executives reported some form of Shadow AI use.
</p>

<p>
  That evidence changes the risk frame. Shadow AI is not only a junior employee compliance issue. It is an executive accountability issue. The people most likely to approve, summarize, direct, or influence corporate output may also be the people most likely to move AI-assisted work outside the organization’s approved evidence system.
</p>

<p>
  The TrustedTech study also found that 76.5% of employees recognize security or data-privacy risks in unapproved AI use, yet usage remains widespread. Nearly one in three employees said they would keep using AI tools even if banned and risking disciplinary action, rising to 37.1% among decision-makers.
</p>

<p>
  Other workforce AI data shows the same disclosure gap from the employee side. An April 2026 ResumeBuilder survey found that 62.8% of workers never told their managers when AI was doing the work, and 60.7% have used AI to absorb tasks once owned by coworkers. An April 2026 AFL-CIO survey found that only 7% of workers say their employer has been transparent about how AI is used to monitor them. Neither side is creating a reliable record of how AI is actually shaping work.
</p>

<p>
  This is why the Name Standard℠ cannot be reduced to software controls or AI policy language. The issue is not only whether the tool was approved. The issue is whether the organization can reconstruct the accountability chain when AI-assisted work appears inside the formal record as ordinary human output.
</p>

<p>
  The Name Standard℠, developed by Lozen Advisory, evaluates whether AI-assisted decisions, approvals, outputs, escalations, and sign-off remain traceable to a responsible human or institutional actor.
</p>

<p>
  This matters when companies rely on AI tools, AI agents, vendor systems, automated workflows, or AI-generated records without a clear evidence model for who reviewed what, what data or source material was used, what was escalated, what was refused, what was corrected, and what remains unresolved.
</p>

<p>
  AI agent governance makes the attribution problem more urgent. Not because agents are people, but because agents act through delegated authority. The question becomes: whose authority is the agent exercising, who authorized the scope, who can revoke it, who reviews the output, and who owns the consequences?
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Agentic AI does not eliminate human attribution. It makes attribution infrastructure mandatory.</strong>
</p>

<p>
  Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ addresses both sides of the silence: the worker who does not disclose AI use, the executive who bypasses approved systems, and the employer whose records were not built to detect what was not disclosed.
</p>

<p>
  Both pillars share an underlying behavioral mechanism — a framework Lozen Advisory defines as Tacere: the individual decision not to disclose.
</p>

  <h2 id="tacere">Tacere: Non-disclosure Performance</h2>

  <p>
    Lozen Advisory’s published research introduces <strong>Tacere</strong> as the individual behavioral complement to Invisible Attrition℠. Tacere is defined as the sustained, strategic practice of keeping one’s own counsel by a senior executive operating in a professional environment where disclosure carries professional risk.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tacere is not a failure condition. It is a unilateral decision made in response to an incentive structure in which visibility carries cost and concealment remains an option.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the AI context, Tacere appears when employees, executives, or technical power users withhold AI use because disclosure may create reputational, performance, surveillance, or career risk. The behavior is not always misconduct. It may be a rational response to an environment where using AI is encouraged rhetorically but penalized evidentially.
  </p>

  <p>
    The existing literature treats non-disclosure as a gap in psychological safety, legal literacy, or organizational support. Tacere establishes that assumption is structurally incomplete. The individual who has accurately assessed the available conditions and determined that disclosure remains professionally untenable is not experiencing a gap in psychological safety. She is exercising a sovereign function over her own information.
  </p>

  <p>
    This has a direct governance implication. Systems built to activate in response to a disclosed condition — accommodation frameworks, wellness programs, clinical pathways, benefit programs — cannot reach the individual who has already decided, on a rational basis, not to disclose. The gap between what those systems can see and what is actually present in the workforce is not a measurement problem that better data collection will close.
  </p>

  <p>
    Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ is designed to address that gap. The governance architecture must function in the presence of Tacere, not in opposition to it.
  </p>

  <p>
    A second mechanism compounds both pillars specifically at the point of AI adoption.
  </p>

  <h2>The Power User Trap℠</h2>

  <p>
    <a href="/frameworks/power-user-trap/"><strong>Power User Trap℠ by Lozen Advisory</strong></a>: employees operating in continuous doubt, constantly verifying and validating AI outputs without organizational recognition of the cognitive burden. It is one mechanism through which Invisible Attrition℠ occurs, where leadership capacity erodes before retention metrics detect risk.
  </p>

  <p>
    This mechanism appears across both workforce and AI governance.
  </p>

  <p>
    In workforce systems, power users may continue absorbing complexity, mentoring, institutional memory, emotional labor, and informal problem-solving while the organization reads their continued output as stability.
  </p>

  <p>
    In AI adoption, power users may become the people who verify AI output, correct errors, repair workflows, identify hallucinations, train colleagues, and prevent failure, even when that work is not formally assigned, measured, staffed, or governed.
  </p>

  <p>
    In an LLM environment, that work often becomes invisible because the organization sees the AI-enabled output but not the human correction, judgment, refusal, and repair labor that made the output usable.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Power User Trap℠ is a Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ problem because the organization may believe it has gained efficiency while actually relocating risk into unmeasured human capacity.
  </p>

<h2>The governance failure disclosure-dependent systems share</h2>

<p>
  The shared failure is not lack of policy. It is lack of evidence.
</p>

<p>
  Disclosure-dependent systems create false assurance when they treat visible process as proof of governed risk. A policy exists. A benefit exists. A survey exists. An AI inventory exists. A human-review workflow exists. But the existence of a process does not prove the organization can see the risk the process is supposed to govern.
</p>

<p>
  In menopause-at-work, the organization may have policies, benefits, surveys, and wellness resources while still failing to detect leaders quietly managing symptoms, stigma, workload, career risk, and capacity loss without disclosure.
</p>

<p>
  In AI governance, the organization may have AI policies, inventories, vendors, approved tools, and review processes while still failing to detect where AI-assisted work entered the record without traceable human review, supported authority, escalation, refusal, or sign-off.
</p>

<p>
  These are not separate failures. They are the same governance architecture failure appearing in two domains. The institution relies on evidence that affected individuals have rational reasons not to create, and then treats the absence of that evidence as absence of risk.
</p>

<p>
  That is the disclosure-dependence gap.
</p>

<p>
  Disclosure-Independent Governance℠ is designed to identify the gap between what the organization can document and what the organization is actually relying on: hidden labor, undisclosed strain, informal workarounds, AI-assisted output, unsupported human review, and accountability that exists in policy but not in evidence.
</p>

<p>
  The result is a widening gap between the official record and operational reality. Left unexamined, that gap produces false assurance for management, weak evidence for boards, and avoidable exposure when the institution is later asked to prove what it knew, what it governed, and who was accountable.
</p>

