  <h2 id="5ws-decision-integrity"> Agentic AI Governance: The 5Ws of Decision Integrity℠</h2>

  <p>
   To maintain robust human-in-the-loop oversight and counter agentic ai automation bias, organizations must evaluate AI-assisted work through The 5Ws of Decision Integrity℠:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>What</strong> data, models, or AI outputs did management rely on, and is that reliance documented?</li>
    <li><strong>Who</strong> held named accountability for this decision, and did that person have the authority and information required to sign off?</li>
    <li><strong>Which</strong> performance indicators were tracked, and what would trigger board escalation if the system degraded or produced biased output?</li>
    <li><strong>What was missed</strong> — what risks, gaps, or AI errors were not detected, and who is responsible for that gap?</li>
    <li><strong>Witness:</strong> What documented record exists that would withstand regulatory scrutiny, litigation, or board inquiry? When an AI error surfaces without a clear owner, Witness is the attribution standard that determines who is responsible and what evidence supports that determination.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The 5Ws of Decision Integrity℠ is a Lozen Advisory framework. It operates at two levels: the board-level question of whether accountability exists, and the management-level record that proves it does.
  </p>

